  "items": [
    {
      "id": "va2-1",
      "level": "A2",
      "num": 1,
      "title": "The imperative and politeness",
      "tr": "Emir kipi ve nezaket",
      "goal": "Give an order, some advice or an instruction, with the informal as well as the formal you.",
      "watch": "emir kipi",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The imperative is built on the verb stem. With the informal you there is often no ending at all: <tur>gel</tur> (come), <tur>git</tur> (go), <tur>oku</tur> (read). With the formal you, add <b>-in / -ın / -un / -ün</b>: <tur>gelin</tur>, <tur>gidin</tur>, <tur>okuyun</tur>."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "The imperative of three common verbs",
          "head": [
            "Infinitive",
            "sen",
            "siz",
            "Negative siz"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "<tur>gelmek</tur>",
              "<tur>gel</tur>",
              "<tur>gelin</tur>",
              "<tur>gelmeyin</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>gitmek</tur>",
              "<tur>git</tur>",
              "<tur>gidin</tur>",
              "<tur>gitmeyin</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>okumak</tur>",
              "<tur>oku</tur>",
              "<tur>okuyun</tur>",
              "<tur>okumayın</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>yemek</tur>",
              "<tur>ye</tur>",
              "<tur>yiyin</tur>",
              "<tur>yemeyin</tur>"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Negation",
          "text": "The negative imperative is formed with <b>-ma / -me</b>: <tur>gelme</tur> (don't come, informal), <tur>gelmeyin</tur> (don't come, formal), <tur>okuma</tur> (don't read), <tur>okumayın</tur>. For the third person: <tur>gelsin</tur> (let him come), <tur>gelmesin</tur> (let him not come)."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "To soften it, add <tur>lütfen</tur> (please) or use the formal you. In speech, <tur>-sana / -sene</tur> makes the request more familiar: <tur>Biraz beklesene.</tur> (Wait a little, would you?)"
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Lütfen kapıyı aç.",
              "fr": "Please open the door."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Biraz bekleyin.",
              "fr": "Wait a little."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Bana yardım edin.",
              "fr": "Help me."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Burada sigara içmeyin.",
              "fr": "Don't smoke here."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "vocab": [
        [
          "açmak",
          "to open"
        ],
        [
          "kapatmak",
          "to close"
        ],
        [
          "beklemek",
          "to wait"
        ],
        [
          "yardım etmek",
          "to help"
        ],
        [
          "sigara içmek",
          "to smoke"
        ],
        [
          "girmek",
          "to enter"
        ],
        [
          "çıkmak",
          "to go out"
        ],
        [
          "oturmak",
          "to sit down"
        ],
        [
          "kalkmak",
          "to get up"
        ],
        [
          "lütfen",
          "please"
        ]
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "What is the polite imperative of « go »?",
          "options": [
            "git",
            "gidin",
            "gitmeyin"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "why": "The formal imperative adds -in: git + in = gidin."
        },
        {
          "q": "How do you say « Don't smoke »?",
          "options": [
            "Sigara içmeyin",
            "Sigara içmiyorsun",
            "Sigara içme"
          ],
          "a": 0,
          "why": "In the second person plural, the negative imperative is -meyin: içmeyin."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "va2-2",
      "level": "A2",
      "num": 2,
      "title": "The optative: « may I… », « let's… »",
      "tr": "İstek kipi",
      "goal": "Suggest an action, express a wish, or say « let me… / let's go ».",
      "watch": "istek kipi",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The optative expresses a wish or a suggestion. Add <b>-a / -e</b> to the stem, then the person markers: <tur>gel-e-yim</tur> (may I come), <tur>gel-e-lim</tur> (let's come), <tur>gel-e-sin</tur> (may you come)."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "The optative of gelmek (to come)",
          "head": [
            "Person",
            "Form",
            "Meaning"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ben",
              "<tur>geleyim</tur>",
              "may I come / let me come"
            ],
            [
              "sen",
              "<tur>gelesin</tur>",
              "may you come"
            ],
            [
              "o",
              "<tur>gele</tur>",
              "may he / she come"
            ],
            [
              "biz",
              "<tur>gelelim</tur>",
              "let's come / let's go"
            ],
            [
              "siz",
              "<tur>gelesiniz</tur>",
              "may you come (plural / formal)"
            ],
            [
              "onlar",
              "<tur>geleler</tur>",
              "may they come (rare)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "The plural in -alım / -elim",
          "text": "The first person plural is the most frequent form: <tur>Gidelim!</tur> (Let's go!), <tur>Başlayalım!</tur> (Let's start!), <tur>Bir çay içelim.</tur> (Let's have a tea.) For the third person plural, <tur>gelsinler</tur> is often preferred."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "To ask a question that makes a suggestion, add <tur>mi</tur>: <tur>Gidelim mi?</tur> (Shall we go?), <tur>Başlayalım mı?</tur> (Shall we start?). The negative is formed with <b>-ma / -me</b>: <tur>gelmeyeyim</tur> (may I not come), <tur>gitmeyelim</tur> (let's not go)."
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Geleyim.",
              "fr": "Let me come."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Gidelim mi?",
              "fr": "Shall we go?"
            },
            {
              "tr": "Bir kahve içelim.",
              "fr": "Let's have a coffee."
            },
            {
              "tr": "İyi olsun.",
              "fr": "May everything go well."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "info",
          "tag": "Don't confuse them",
          "text": "<tur>Gidelim</tur> = « let's go » (optative). <tur>Gitmeliyim</tur> = « I have to go » (necessity). The suffix <b>-malı</b> adds an obligation that the optative does not have."
        }
      ],
      "vocab": [
        [
          "gelmek",
          "to come"
        ],
        [
          "gitmek",
          "to go"
        ],
        [
          "başlamak",
          "to start"
        ],
        [
          "içmek",
          "to drink"
        ],
        [
          "yapmak",
          "to do"
        ],
        [
          "olmak",
          "to be, to become"
        ],
        [
          "birlikte",
          "together"
        ],
        [
          "hadi",
          "come on"
        ],
        [
          "belki",
          "maybe"
        ],
        [
          "dilemek",
          "to wish"
        ]
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "What does « Gidelim » mean?",
          "options": [
            "I have to go",
            "Let's go",
            "He went"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "why": "The first person plural of the optative expresses a suggestion: let's go."
        },
        {
          "q": "How do you say « Shall we start? »?",
          "options": [
            "Başlayalım mı?",
            "Başlamalıyım?",
            "Başladık mı?"
          ],
          "a": 0,
          "why": "Başlayalım is the optative « let's start »; with mi, you suggest starting."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "va2-3",
      "level": "A2",
      "num": 3,
      "title": "Ability: -abil / -ebil",
      "tr": "-abilmek / -ebilmek",
      "goal": "Say what you can do, ask for permission and express possibility.",
      "watch": "-abilmek -ebilmek",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "To express ability or possibility, insert <b>-abil / -ebil</b> between the stem and the ending: <tur>oku-yabil-ir-im</tur> (I can read), <tur>gel-ebil-ir-sin</tur> (you can come). The choice of vowel follows vowel harmony."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "Ability in the present",
          "head": [
            "Person",
            "okumak (to read)",
            "gelmek (to come)"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "ben",
              "<tur>okuyabilirim</tur>",
              "<tur>gelebilirim</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "sen",
              "<tur>okuyabilirsin</tur>",
              "<tur>gelebilirsin</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "o",
              "<tur>okuyabilir</tur>",
              "<tur>gelebilir</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "biz",
              "<tur>okuyabiliriz</tur>",
              "<tur>gelebiliriz</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "siz",
              "<tur>okuyabilirsiniz</tur>",
              "<tur>gelebilirsiniz</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "onlar",
              "<tur>okuyabilirler</tur>",
              "<tur>gelebilirler</tur>"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "The negative is irregular",
          "text": "Be careful: <tur>okumayabilirim</tur> exists, but it means « it is possible that I don't read ». For « I cannot read », use <tur>okuyamam</tur>, then <tur>okuyamazsın</tur>, <tur>okuyamaz</tur>, <tur>okuyamayız</tur>, <tur>okuyamazsınız</tur>, <tur>okuyamazlar</tur>. The same logic applies to <tur>gelemem</tur> (I cannot come)."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "<b>-abilir</b> can also express permission or probability: <tur>Burada oturabilir miyim?</tur> (May I sit here?), <tur>Yarın yağmur yağabilir.</tur> (It might rain tomorrow.) Context decides between ability, permission and possibility."
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Türkçe okuyabiliyorum.",
              "fr": "I can read Turkish."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Yarın gelebilir miyim?",
              "fr": "May I come tomorrow?"
            },
            {
              "tr": "Burada sigara içemezsiniz.",
              "fr": "You cannot smoke here."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Onu anlayabiliyorum.",
              "fr": "I can understand it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "info",
          "tag": "bilmek",
          "text": "<tur>Bilmek</tur> on its own means « to know »: <tur>Türkçe biliyorum.</tur> (I know Turkish.) With <b>-abil</b>, it becomes a suffix of ability: <tur>Türkçe konuşabiliyorum.</tur> (I can speak Turkish.)"
        }
      ],
      "vocab": [
        [
          "okumak",
          "to read"
        ],
        [
          "yazmak",
          "to write"
        ],
        [
          "konuşmak",
          "to speak"
        ],
        [
          "anlamak",
          "to understand"
        ],
        [
          "gelmek",
          "to come"
        ],
        [
          "gitmek",
          "to go"
        ],
        [
          "oturmak",
          "to sit down"
        ],
        [
          "izin",
          "permission"
        ],
        [
          "belki",
          "maybe"
        ],
        [
          "bilmek",
          "to know"
        ]
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "How do you say « I cannot come »?",
          "options": [
            "Gelemiyorum",
            "Gelemem",
            "Gelmiyorum"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "why": "The negative ability form of gelmek is gelemem (I cannot come); gelmiyorum simply means « I am not coming »."
        },
        {
          "q": "What does « Burada oturabilir miyim? » mean?",
          "options": [
            "Should I sit here?",
            "May I sit here?",
            "Am I sitting here?"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "why": "-abilir + miyim expresses permission: may I sit here?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "va2-4",
      "level": "A2",
      "num": 4,
      "title": "Gerunds: -arak and -ıp",
      "tr": "-arak / -erek ve -ıp / -ip",
      "goal": "Link two actions: say « by doing » and « do, then… ».",
      "watch": "-arak -erek -ıp -ip",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The suffix <b>-arak / -erek</b> means « by doing »: it describes how the main action is carried out. <tur>Koşarak geldi.</tur> (He came running.) <tur>Gülerek cevap verdi.</tur> (She answered with a smile.)"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "The suffix <b>-ıp / -ip / -up / -üp</b> links two successive actions of the same subject: <tur>Kitabı okuyup özetledi.</tur> (He read the book and then summarised it.) <tur>Otobüse binip eve gitti.</tur> (He took the bus and then went home.)"
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "Forming the two gerunds",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "-arak / -erek",
            "-ıp / -ip"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "<tur>gelmek</tur>",
              "<tur>gelerek</tur>",
              "<tur>gelip</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>gitmek</tur>",
              "<tur>giderek</tur>",
              "<tur>gidip</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>okumak</tur>",
              "<tur>okuyarak</tur>",
              "<tur>okuyup</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>yapmak</tur>",
              "<tur>yaparak</tur>",
              "<tur>yapıp</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>görmek</tur>",
              "<tur>görerek</tur>",
              "<tur>görüp</tur>"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "Same subject",
          "text": "Both actions must have the same subject. <tur>Ali gelip masayı temizledi.</tur> = Ali came and cleaned the table. If the subjects differ, use a full clause with <tur>ve</tur> or another connector."
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Koşarak geldi.",
              "fr": "He came running."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Gülerek cevap verdi.",
              "fr": "She answered with a smile."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Kitabı okuyup özetledi.",
              "fr": "He read the book and then summarised it."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Otobüse binip eve gitti.",
              "fr": "He took the bus and then went home."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "info",
          "tag": "A spelling detail",
          "text": "When the stem ends in a vowel, <tur>okumak</tur> takes a linking <b>y</b> before -arak: <tur>okuyarak</tur>. Before -ıp, the final vowel of the stem stays: <tur>oku-y-up</tur>."
        }
      ],
      "vocab": [
        [
          "koşmak",
          "to run"
        ],
        [
          "yürümek",
          "to walk"
        ],
        [
          "gülmek",
          "to laugh"
        ],
        [
          "bakmak",
          "to look"
        ],
        [
          "binmek",
          "to get on"
        ],
        [
          "inmek",
          "to get off"
        ],
        [
          "özetlemek",
          "to summarise"
        ],
        [
          "cevap vermek",
          "to answer"
        ],
        [
          "temizlemek",
          "to clean"
        ],
        [
          "hazırlamak",
          "to prepare"
        ]
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {
          "q": "What does « Koşarak geldi » mean?",
          "options": [
            "He came running",
            "He ran and then came",
            "He often runs"
          ],
          "a": 0,
          "why": "-arak describes the manner: he came running."
        },
        {
          "q": "Which form links « read » and then « summarise »?",
          "options": [
            "okuyarak",
            "okuyup",
            "okumak"
          ],
          "a": 1,
          "why": "-ıp / -ip marks succession: okuyup = having read, then."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "va2-5",
      "level": "A2",
      "num": 5,
      "title": "Verbal nouns: -mak, -ma, -ış",
      "tr": "İsim-fiiller",
      "goal": "Turn a verb into a noun to talk about an activity, a preference or a manner.",
      "watch": "isim fiil -mak -ma -ış",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Turkish can turn a verb into a noun in several ways. <b>-mak / -mek</b> gives the infinitive-noun: <tur>yüzmek</tur> (to swim / swimming). <b>-ma / -me</b> gives the action noun: <tur>yüzme</tur> (swimming, the act of swimming). <b>-ış / -iş</b> often gives the manner: <tur>yürüyüş</tur> (walking, the way of walking)."
        },
        {
          "t": "table",
          "caption": "Three nominalisations of common verbs",
          "head": [
            "Verb",
            "-mak / -mek",
            "-ma / -me",
            "-ış / -iş"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "<tur>okumak</tur>",
              "<tur>okumak</tur>",
              "<tur>okuma</tur>",
              "<tur>okuyuş</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>yüzmek</tur>",
              "<tur>yüzmek</tur>",
              "<tur>yüzme</tur>",
              "<tur>yüzüş</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>gelmek</tur>",
              "<tur>gelmek</tur>",
              "<tur>gelme</tur>",
              "<tur>geliş</tur>"
            ],
            [
              "<tur>gülmek</tur>",
              "<tur>gülmek</tur>",
              "<tur>gülme</tur>",
              "<tur>gülüş</tur>"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "<b>-mak</b> is used in particular with <tur>istemek</tur> (to want) and <tur>için</tur> (for): <tur>Türkçe öğrenmek istiyorum.</tur> (I want to learn Turkish.) <tur>Kitap okumak için zamanım yok.</tur> (I don't have time to read.)"
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "<b>-ma</b> takes case suffixes: <tur>Okumayı seviyorum.</tur> (I like reading.) <tur>Yüzmeyi öğreniyorum.</tur> (I am learning to swim.) <tur>Gelmeyi unuttum.</tur> (I forgot to come.) The suffix -ma becomes -ma-yı in the accusative."
        },
        {
          "t": "ex",
          "items": [
            {
              "tr": "Türkçe öğrenmek istiyorum.",
              "fr": "I want to learn Turkish."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Yüzmeyi seviyorum.",
              "fr": "I like swimming."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Konuşması çok güzel.",
              "fr": "Her way of speaking is very nice."
            },
            {
              "tr": "Kitap okumak için zamanım yok.",
              "fr": "I don't have time to read."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "t": "note",
          "kind": "tip",
          "tag": "-ış is not always regular",
          "text": "Some nouns in <b>-ış</b> have become words in their own right: <tur>yürüyüş</tur> (walk, stroll), <tur>bakış</tur> (look, gaze), <tur>gülüş</tur> (laugh), <tur>anlatış</tur> (way of telling). Learn them as vocabulary."
        }
      ],
      "vocab": [
        [
          "yüzmek",
          "to swim"
        ],
        [
          "okumak",
          "to read"
        ],
        [
          "öğrenmek",
          "to learn"
        ],
        [
          "sevmek",
          "to like"
        ],
        [
          "istemek",
          "to want"
        ],
